# ===== TOPIC 29: Pediatric Neuroanesthesia =====
{"id":"pedi-neuroanesthesia-d1","topic":"Pediatric Neuroanesthesia","domain":"Neuroanesthesia (intracranial pressure & cerebral physiology, craniotomy, awake craniotomy, spine surgery, neuromonitoring, TBI, aneurysm)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"A major concern within anesthesiology is the impact of general anesthesia on neurodevelopment; what is the current state of evidence?","answer":"Although definitive conclusions cannot be made, a major concern exists about the impact of general anesthesia and sedative drugs on neurodevelopment and cognition across the life span, particularly for very young children.","rationale":"Animal studies show apoptotic neurodegeneration with anesthetic exposure; human data are confounded by the surgical condition requiring anesthesia; single brief exposures appear to carry minimal clinical risk.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Miller/Baby Miller","page":209}],"confusable_with":"anesthesia neurotoxicity being definitively proven in humans (current evidence is insufficient to confirm clinically significant neurotoxicity from brief, single exposures in children)"},
{"id":"pedi-neuroanesthesia-d2","topic":"Pediatric Neuroanesthesia","domain":"Neuroanesthesia (intracranial pressure & cerebral physiology, craniotomy, awake craniotomy, spine surgery, neuromonitoring, TBI, aneurysm)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Parental presence during induction of anesthesia (PPIA) is offered based on what assessment?","answer":"PPIA requires assessment of both the child's temperament and the parent's temperament; a calm, cooperative parent reduces child anxiety but a distressed parent may worsen it; institutional protocols and individual assessment guide the decision.","rationale":"Parental presence can reduce preoperative anxiety in cooperative parents and temperamentally anxious children; however, parental anxiety itself can be transmitted to the child, potentially causing physiologic responses including syncope.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Miller/Baby Miller","page":647}],"confusable_with":"PPIA benefiting all children universally (temperament-dependent; not universally beneficial)"},
{"id":"pedi-neuroanesthesia-d3","topic":"Pediatric Neuroanesthesia","domain":"Neuroanesthesia (intracranial pressure & cerebral physiology, craniotomy, awake craniotomy, spine surgery, neuromonitoring, TBI, aneurysm)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Major contraindications to neuraxial anesthesia include which conditions?","answer":"Major contraindications to neuraxial anesthesia include lack of consent, coagulation abnormalities, severe hypovolemia, elevated intracranial pressure, and infection at the injection site.","rationale":"Elevated ICP is a neuraxial contraindication because spinal or epidural injection can cause transtentorial herniation from sudden CSF pressure change; coagulopathy risks epidural hematoma.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":1541}],"confusable_with":"relative contraindications (e.g., anticoagulation on prophylactic dosing, localized skin infection remote from injection site are relative, not absolute)"},
{"id":"pedi-neuroanesthesia-d4","topic":"Pediatric Neuroanesthesia","domain":"Neuroanesthesia (intracranial pressure & cerebral physiology, craniotomy, awake craniotomy, spine surgery, neuromonitoring, TBI, aneurysm)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Neonates with a history of prematurity and post-conceptual age <60 weeks may have what specific postoperative complication after anesthesia?","answer":"Neonates with prematurity and post-conceptual age <60 weeks may have postoperative apnea episodes; they require admission for monitoring rather than outpatient discharge after general anesthesia.","rationale":"Immature ventilatory control systems in ex-premature neonates are further depressed by anesthetic agents; postoperative apnea events occur in the first 12-24 hours and can be life-threatening without monitoring.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Miller/Baby Miller","page":91}],"confusable_with":"term neonates (also at increased risk but the threshold for admission is specifically ex-premature infants with PCA <60 weeks)"},
{"id":"pedi-neuroanesthesia-d5","topic":"Pediatric Neuroanesthesia","domain":"Neuroanesthesia (intracranial pressure & cerebral physiology, craniotomy, awake craniotomy, spine surgery, neuromonitoring, TBI, aneurysm)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"The MAC of halogenated agents in infants is greater than in neonates and adults except for one agent; which agent shows no increase in infants versus neonates?","answer":"No increase in MAC of sevoflurane is demonstrated between neonates and infants (unlike other halogenated agents which peak in infancy); nitrous oxide does not reduce MAC of desflurane or sevoflurane in neonates and infants.","rationale":"The MAC-age relationship peaks in infancy for most volatile agents (due to highest neuronal density and synaptic activity); sevoflurane's profile is unique — its MAC is similar in neonates and infants.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":1455}],"confusable_with":"sevoflurane MAC being lower in infants than adults (it is similar in neonates and infants; adult MAC is lower than infant MAC for most agents)"},
{"id":"pedi-neuroanesthesia-d6","topic":"Pediatric Neuroanesthesia","domain":"Neuroanesthesia (intracranial pressure & cerebral physiology, craniotomy, awake craniotomy, spine surgery, neuromonitoring, TBI, aneurysm)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage and intracranial aneurysms are managed by neuroanesthesiologists; what is the cardinal principle for ICP and blood pressure management?","answer":"The cardinal principle is to maintain CPP (MAP - ICP) within safe limits while avoiding hypertension before aneurysm clip application (aneurysm re-rupture risk) and maintaining it after clipping (vasospasm prevention).","rationale":"Pre-clip hypertension risks aneurysm rupture; post-clip hypotension risks cerebral ischemia from vasospasm; the management goal shifts fundamentally before versus after aneurysm securing.","bloom":"analyze","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":2334}],"confusable_with":"single BP target throughout the case (the target changes critically at the moment of clip application)"},

# ===== TOPIC 30: Perioperative MI =====
{"id":"periop-mi-d1","topic":"Perioperative Myocardial Infarction — Recognition & Management","domain":"Cardiac anesthesia (valvular heart disease, CAD/ischemia, cardiac output, arrhythmias, pacemakers/ICDs, cardiopulmonary bypass, cardiac surgery specifics)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"In cardiac transplant recipients undergoing non-cardiac surgery, what makes perioperative MI recognition particularly difficult?","answer":"After cardiac transplantation, the heart is denervated; myocardial ischemia and infarction are almost always silent (no chest pain) because cardiac sympathetic afferents are absent.","rationale":"Transplant recipients lack the pain sensation that normally warns of ischemia; therefore, ECG monitoring, troponin surveillance, and indirect evidence (new wall motion abnormalities, hemodynamic changes) are the only warning signs.","bloom":"analyze","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":693}],"confusable_with":"standard silent MI risk in diabetics (also common due to autonomic neuropathy; transplant denervation causes universal silent MI in all recipients)"},
{"id":"periop-mi-d2","topic":"Perioperative Myocardial Infarction — Recognition & Management","domain":"Cardiac anesthesia (valvular heart disease, CAD/ischemia, cardiac output, arrhythmias, pacemakers/ICDs, cardiopulmonary bypass, cardiac surgery specifics)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"In the stepwise ACC/AHA approach to perioperative cardiac assessment, what is the first step for patients scheduled for surgery?","answer":"Step 1: In patients scheduled for surgery with known or suspected coronary artery disease, determine if the urgency of surgery allows time for further cardiac evaluation; emergency surgery proceeds without delay for cardiac workup.","rationale":"Urgent/emergent surgery proceeds with optimization of available data; elective surgery allows formal risk stratification; the stepwise algorithm prevents unnecessary delays for low-risk patients and low-risk procedures.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":622}],"confusable_with":"always performing stress testing before surgery in cardiac patients (Step 1 filters for urgency; most patients do not require testing before appropriately risk-stratified procedures)"},
{"id":"periop-mi-d3","topic":"Perioperative Myocardial Infarction — Recognition & Management","domain":"Cardiac anesthesia (valvular heart disease, CAD/ischemia, cardiac output, arrhythmias, pacemakers/ICDs, cardiopulmonary bypass, cardiac surgery specifics)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Conditions associated with increased perioperative cardiac risk include which four major categories?","answer":"Increased perioperative cardiac risk is associated with: ischemic heart disease (MI history, ECG evidence), congestive heart failure (dyspnea, pulmonary edema), cerebrovascular disease (stroke/TIA), and diabetes mellitus/renal insufficiency.","rationale":"These conditions form the basis of the Revised Cardiac Risk Index (RCRI); each adds incremental risk for MACE (major adverse cardiac events) perioperatively.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":619}],"confusable_with":"hypertension as a major risk factor (hypertension is a risk factor for CAD but is not independently a major perioperative cardiac risk factor at mild-moderate severity)"},
{"id":"periop-mi-d4","topic":"Perioperative Myocardial Infarction — Recognition & Management","domain":"Cardiac anesthesia (valvular heart disease, CAD/ischemia, cardiac output, arrhythmias, pacemakers/ICDs, cardiopulmonary bypass, cardiac surgery specifics)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"A preoperative ECG shows evidence of a prior infarction; does this exclude significant coronary artery disease or LVH?","answer":"A normal ECG does not exclude CAD or LVH; conversely, an abnormal ECG showing ischemia, conduction abnormalities, or old infarction provides additional risk information. Similarly, normal heart size on CXR does not exclude LVH.","rationale":"Resting ECG has low sensitivity for CAD; it identifies established changes but misses unstable plaques, submaximal ischemia, and early LVH; it supplements but does not replace clinical risk stratification.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":631}],"confusable_with":"relying on normal ECG to clear a high-risk patient for cardiac surgery (normal ECG does not provide cardiac clearance)"},
{"id":"periop-mi-d5","topic":"Perioperative Myocardial Infarction — Recognition & Management","domain":"Cardiac anesthesia (valvular heart disease, CAD/ischemia, cardiac output, arrhythmias, pacemakers/ICDs, cardiopulmonary bypass, cardiac surgery specifics)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Nebivolol is distinguished from other beta-blockers by what unique mechanism?","answer":"Nebivolol is a newer-generation beta-1 selective blocker that uniquely causes direct vasodilation via stimulation of endothelial nitric oxide synthesis, in addition to beta-1 blockade.","rationale":"Most beta-blockers reduce cardiac output; nebivolol's additional NO-mediated vasodilation reduces afterload and can improve forward flow, distinguishing its hemodynamic profile from other beta-blockers.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":398}],"confusable_with":"carvedilol (also vasodilatory via alpha-1 blockade, not NO; nebivolol uses a distinct endothelial mechanism)"},
{"id":"periop-mi-d6","topic":"Perioperative Myocardial Infarction — Recognition & Management","domain":"Cardiac anesthesia (valvular heart disease, CAD/ischemia, cardiac output, arrhythmias, pacemakers/ICDs, cardiopulmonary bypass, cardiac surgery specifics)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Controlled hypotension is used during which specific neurosurgical and orthopedic procedures?","answer":"Controlled hypotension is used during cerebral aneurysm repair (to reduce transmural pressure), brain tumor resection, total hip replacement, and spinal fusion to reduce operative blood loss.","rationale":"Deliberate MAP reduction to 50-65 mmHg reduces bleeding in vascular beds; cerebral aneurysm repair benefits from reduced wall tension during dissection; orthopedic procedures benefit from reduced surgical field bleeding.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":414}],"confusable_with":"unintentional hypotension (controlled hypotension is deliberate with a target MAP; unintentional hypotension in these patients is a complication, not a technique)"},

# ===== TOPIC 31: Perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis =====
{"id":"periop-abx-d1","topic":"Perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis","domain":"Patient safety, ethics, professionalism & quality (closed-claims lessons, never events, root-cause analysis, informed consent, communication, handoffs, wellness)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"What are the two key elements of antibiotic prophylaxis that reduce surgical site infection risk?","answer":"Appropriate selection AND timing of preoperative antibiotic prophylaxis reduce SSI risk; both elements are independently critical — the right antibiotic given at the wrong time provides insufficient protection.","rationale":"Peak tissue antibiotic concentration must coincide with bacterial inoculation at skin incision; both agent spectrum coverage and peak tissue timing are independently required for efficacy.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":1820}],"confusable_with":"prolonged postoperative antibiotic courses reducing SSI (restricting antibiotics to the immediate perioperative period is equivalent or superior to prolonged courses)"},
{"id":"periop-abx-d2","topic":"Perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis","domain":"Patient safety, ethics, professionalism & quality (closed-claims lessons, never events, root-cause analysis, informed consent, communication, handoffs, wellness)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Surgical site infection was found to be the most common adverse surgical event in what landmark study?","answer":"A 1984 study in 51 acute care hospitals in New York State found SSI was the most common adverse surgical event and the second most common adverse event overall (after medication errors).","rationale":"This study established SSI prevention as a major quality target; subsequent evidence showed that appropriate antibiotic prophylaxis is the single most effective preventive intervention.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Stanford CA-1","page":91}],"confusable_with":"falls or medication errors being the leading surgical adverse event (medication errors were #1 overall; SSI was #1 among surgical adverse events specifically)"},
{"id":"periop-abx-d3","topic":"Perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis","domain":"Patient safety, ethics, professionalism & quality (closed-claims lessons, never events, root-cause analysis, informed consent, communication, handoffs, wellness)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"For cefazolin perioperative prophylaxis, what dose is given to patients >120 kg and what is the standard adult dose?","answer":"Standard adult cefazolin dose is 2g IV; patients >120 kg should receive 3g IV; pediatric dosing is 30 mg/kg (max 2g); it can be bolused over 3 minutes or given over 30 minutes.","rationale":"Weight-based dosing adjustments ensure adequate tissue concentrations in obese patients; standard dosing may produce subtherapeutic tissue levels in patients >80-120 kg.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Stanford CA-1","page":92}],"confusable_with":"2g for all adults regardless of weight (inadequate for obese patients; 3g is needed for >120 kg)"},
{"id":"periop-abx-d4","topic":"Perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis","domain":"Patient safety, ethics, professionalism & quality (closed-claims lessons, never events, root-cause analysis, informed consent, communication, handoffs, wellness)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Restricting antibiotic prophylaxis to what time frame decreases incidence of postoperative infections?","answer":"Restricting antibiotic prophylaxis to the immediate perioperative period (not prolonged post-op courses) decreases the incidence of postoperative infections by reducing selective pressure for resistant organisms.","rationale":"Prolonged antibiotic courses promote resistant flora (C. difficile, MRSA, VRE) without additional SSI benefit; single perioperative doses with re-dosing for prolonged cases provide equivalent protection.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":2152}],"confusable_with":"24-48 hour postoperative antibiotic courses for all surgical patients (evidence does not support prolonged prophylaxis; restriction to perioperative window is current standard)"},
{"id":"periop-abx-d5","topic":"Perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis","domain":"Patient safety, ethics, professionalism & quality (closed-claims lessons, never events, root-cause analysis, informed consent, communication, handoffs, wellness)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Perioperative maintenance of normothermia is important for what outcome beyond the surgical stress response?","answer":"Normothermia maintenance reduces SSI risk (hypothermia impairs leukocyte killing and reduces oxygen delivery to wounds), reduces transfusion requirements, prevents coagulopathy, and decreases myocardial complications.","rationale":"Perioperative hypothermia causes multiple harms: impaired platelet aggregation, impaired leukocyte function, increased catecholamine release; maintaining normothermia addresses all these simultaneously.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":1820}],"confusable_with":"normothermia being relevant only for comfort (it is a quantified clinical intervention with measurable outcome impacts beyond comfort)"},
{"id":"periop-abx-d6","topic":"Perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis","domain":"Patient safety, ethics, professionalism & quality (closed-claims lessons, never events, root-cause analysis, informed consent, communication, handoffs, wellness)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Vancomycin is given for perioperative prophylaxis in penicillin-allergic patients; what is the dosing and timing requirement?","answer":"Vancomycin: 15 mg/kg IV (adult dosing by weight; common range 1-2g); give over 30-60 min or slower than 10 mg/min (to prevent Red Man syndrome); can be started 60-120 min prior to incision.","rationale":"Vancomycin's required slow infusion means it must be started earlier than beta-lactams; too-rapid infusion causes histamine release (Red Man syndrome) with flushing and hypotension.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Stanford CA-1","page":92}],"confusable_with":"cefazolin timing (given 30-60 min before incision; vancomycin requires 60-120 min because of the slow infusion requirement)"},

# ===== TOPIC 32: Perioperative anticoagulation management =====
{"id":"periop-anticoag-d1","topic":"Perioperative anticoagulation management","domain":"Perioperative medicine & systems (enhanced recovery, preoperative optimization, geriatric anesthesia, multimodal teams, handoffs, triage)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Perioperative management of patients on anticoagulation requires balancing what two competing risks?","answer":"Perioperative anticoagulation management balances the risk of surgical bleeding (if anticoagulation is continued) against the risk of postoperative thromboembolism (if anticoagulation is held).","rationale":"The optimal perioperative strategy depends on the indication for anticoagulation (AF, DVT, mechanical valve), the thrombotic risk of stopping, and the bleeding risk of the surgery.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Miller/Baby Miller","page":456}],"confusable_with":"always stopping anticoagulation for surgery (high thrombotic risk patients — e.g., mechanical mitral valve — may require bridging anticoagulation)"},
{"id":"periop-anticoag-d2","topic":"Perioperative anticoagulation management","domain":"Perioperative medicine & systems (enhanced recovery, preoperative optimization, geriatric anesthesia, multimodal teams, handoffs, triage)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"High bleeding risk procedures include what specific categories?","answer":"High bleeding risk procedures (30-day major bleed risk >2%) include major surgery with extensive tissue injury, cancer surgery (especially solid tumor resection), major orthopedic surgery (including shoulder replacement), and reconstructive plastic surgery.","rationale":"Tissue factor exposure and surgical vascularity determine bleeding risk; high-risk procedures require anticoagulation holding and careful bridging decisions.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Miller/Baby Miller","page":228}],"confusable_with":"any surgery requiring general anesthesia as high bleeding risk (minor procedures like skin biopsy are low bleeding risk even under general anesthesia)"},
{"id":"periop-anticoag-d3","topic":"Perioperative anticoagulation management","domain":"Perioperative medicine & systems (enhanced recovery, preoperative optimization, geriatric anesthesia, multimodal teams, handoffs, triage)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"The incidence of spinal hematoma with epidural block is estimated at approximately what rate?","answer":"The incidence of spinal hematoma is approximately 1 in 150,000 for epidural blocks and 1 in 220,000 for spinal blocks; the introduction of LMWH substantially increased this risk when timing guidelines were not followed.","rationale":"LMWH's introduction in Europe without established timing guidelines led to epidural hematoma cases; ASRA guidelines were subsequently developed specifying minimum intervals between LMWH dosing and neuraxial placement.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Miller/Baby Miller","page":751}],"confusable_with":"the risk being negligible with modern techniques (rare but catastrophic complication; the estimated incidence drives the strict ASRA timing recommendations)"},
{"id":"periop-anticoag-d4","topic":"Perioperative anticoagulation management","domain":"Perioperative medicine & systems (enhanced recovery, preoperative optimization, geriatric anesthesia, multimodal teams, handoffs, triage)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"The HAS-BLED score is used for what perioperative purpose?","answer":"HAS-BLED stratifies bleeding risk with oral anticoagulation in AF patients; high score (>=3) indicates high bleeding risk; components include HTN (SBP>160), abnormal renal/liver function, stroke history, bleeding history, labile INR, elderly (>65), and drugs/alcohol.","rationale":"HAS-BLED helps guide anticoagulation decisions by quantifying modifiable and non-modifiable bleeding risk factors; it complements CHA2DS2-VASc for the bleeding-clot balance.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"MGH Housestaff Manual","page":14}],"confusable_with":"CHA2DS2-VASc (stratifies stroke risk to guide anticoagulation initiation; HAS-BLED stratifies bleeding risk)"},
{"id":"periop-anticoag-d5","topic":"Perioperative anticoagulation management","domain":"Perioperative medicine & systems (enhanced recovery, preoperative optimization, geriatric anesthesia, multimodal teams, handoffs, triage)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) is used in what clinical context, and what is the key consideration for perioperative management?","answer":"DAPT is used after PCI (coronary stent placement) and ACS to prevent stent thrombosis; premature discontinuation risks catastrophic in-stent thrombosis; elective surgery should be deferred until the minimum DAPT duration is completed.","rationale":"Drug-eluting stents require DAPT (aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor) for at least 6-12 months; premature discontinuation causes stent thrombosis with acute MI risk; the risk-benefit must be assessed with the cardiologist.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Miller/Baby Miller","page":229}],"confusable_with":"stopping DAPT for any surgical bleeding risk (for stented patients, stopping DAPT before the minimum period carries higher mortality than the surgical bleeding risk for most non-cardiac surgeries)"},
{"id":"periop-anticoag-d6","topic":"Perioperative anticoagulation management","domain":"Perioperative medicine & systems (enhanced recovery, preoperative optimization, geriatric anesthesia, multimodal teams, handoffs, triage)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Because neuraxial anesthesia is commonly used in obstetric patients on antithrombotic agents, what timing consideration is critically important?","answer":"Appropriate timing of antithrombotic agents before and after neuraxial placement is critically important to minimize the risk of epidural hematoma; international recommendations specify minimum intervals (e.g., 12h after prophylactic LMWH, 24h after therapeutic LMWH).","rationale":"Neuraxial hematoma in a pregnant patient causes cord compression and paraplegia; strict adherence to ASRA and international neuraxial timing guidelines is the primary preventive measure.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":1820}],"confusable_with":"treating antithrombotic agents identically for all patients (obstetric patients have specific risk profiles and the combination of pregnancy hypercoagulability and neuraxial use creates unique requirements)"},
