{"_type":"illness_script","topic":"Perioperative Myocardial Infarction — Recognition & Management","discipline":"anesthesia","enabling_conditions":"Pre-existing CAD, cardiac transplant (denervated heart), perioperative hemodynamic stress, coagulopathy from major blood loss","pathophysiology":"Type 1: Plaque rupture and thrombosis (supply-demand mismatch); Type 2: Supply-demand mismatch without plaque rupture (tachycardia, hypotension, anemia); both elevated troponin but different management","time_course":"Most perioperative MIs occur in first 48-72h postoperatively; peak at 3 days; transplant recipients have no warning symptoms at any time","key_features":"Asymptomatic (transplant, DM neuropathy), new ST changes, hemodynamic instability, elevated troponin; ECG may be unreliable in LBBB or LVH","consequence_if_missed":"Untreated perioperative MI has high mortality; delayed revascularization in type 1 MI worsens outcome; Type 2 MI requires hemodynamic optimization not PCI"}
